        {
          "t": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Vision Insurance vs. Medical Insurance",
          "html": "Vision Insurance vs. Medical Insurance",
          "id": "LRDi16vtq9",
          "col": 1,
          "origin": "static"
        },
        {
          "t": "html",
          "html": "<p><strong>Vision insurance</strong> is typically used for routine care. This includes annual <a href=\"/eye-care-services/comprehensive-eye-exams/\">eye exams</a>, prescription updates, and help covering the cost of glasses or contact lenses. If you’re coming in for a regular checkup or need new eyewear, this is usually the plan that applies.</p><p><strong>Medical insurance </strong>comes into play when there’s a health-related concern with your eyes. This can include things like infections, dry eye, eye disease, or sudden changes in your vision. If your visit is focused on treating a condition or addressing a specific issue, medical insurance is often used instead.</p>",
          "id": "sssjgiNTqw",
          "col": 1,
          "origin": "static"
        }
      ]
    },
